# NOTES
# Tooling constraints across all three research passes: NRC.gov (docket/FSAR/info-finder pages) returned HTTP 403 to WebFetch on every attempt; Global Energy Monitor (gem.wiki) and Grokipedia also 403'd. WebSearch quota was exhausted mid-task in all three groups, cutting off further primary-source lookups (especially for Point Beach, Quad Cities, River Bend, Sequoyah, Shippingport AE/turbine/RPV fields). This means many UNVERIFIABLE verdicts reflect blocked tooling, not necessarily absence of real documentation — a follow-up pass with NRC ADAMS access via browser (not automated WebFetch) or a fresh WebSearch budget would likely resolve a meaningful fraction of them.
# Wikipedia's "Infobox nuclear power station" template does not carry constructor/architect_engineer/turbine_supplier/rpv_manufacturer parameters on any of the Point Beach, Prairie Island, Quad Cities, Rancho Seco, or River Bend articles (confirmed via raw wikitext) — these fields are structurally absent from Wikipedia for that plant cluster, not merely unresearched.
# power-technology.com was used as a LOW-confidence sole source for several BWR plants (Peach Bottom 2/3, Perry) and for the San Onofre/Salem/Seabrook cluster. Per the known trap, it sometimes names the NSSS vendor as "reactor vessel supplier" even when that is not the actual fabricator (e.g., Peach Bottom's claimed rpv_manufacturer=Babcock & Wilcox is distinct from GE's NSSS role, and Salem's claimed rpv_manufacturer=Combustion Engineering is distinct from Westinghouse's NSSS role — both cases where the site's number is NOT simply the NSSS vendor, which somewhat mitigates conflation risk. San Onofre 2/3's rpv_manufacturer claim (Combustion Engineering) exactly IS the NSSS vendor, so it was marked UNVERIFIABLE rather than CONFIRMED to avoid the conflation trap.
# Significant finding: San Onofre units 1/2/3 constructor was claimed as Westinghouse (unit 1) / CE (units 2,3) but power-technology.com states all three units were built by Bechtel — constructor and architect_engineer were swapped/conflated with the NSSS vendor across the whole plant. Similarly Palo Verde 3, Pilgrim 1, and Rancho Seco 1 each had "constructor" wrongly set to the NSSS vendor (CE, GE, GE respectively) instead of the actual construction firm (Bechtel in all three cases) — suggesting the original heuristic systematically confused NSSS vendor with constructor across the batch.
# Saxton 1 is a clean refutation: Westinghouse was NOT merely the NSSS/turbine vendor for this prototype — Wikipedia states Westinghouse designed AND constructed the reactor, contradicting the claimed constructor=GE, and the plant had no dedicated turbine (shared the adjacent coal plant's), contradicting nothing since turbine_supplier was already claimed as None — that part was correct.
# Robinson 2's claimed constructor=Westinghouse and architect_engineer=Ebasco: research indicates Ebasco Services held both the AE and constructor role (not Westinghouse as constructor), consistent with the systematic NSSS-vendor-as-constructor conflation pattern noted above.
